On the set theory (see \cite{set-theory} for instance), one defines natural
numbers by following:
$$
    0 \defeq \emptyset,\quad 1 \defeq \{0\},\quad 
    2 \defeq \{0,1\},\quad 3 \defeq \{0,1,2\}, ... .
$$
For their definitions, if $n$ is a natural number, we can observe the following
facts, their rigorous proofs is written in \cite{set-theory}.
#enum ((\alph*)) {
    \item For any natural number $n$, every element of $n$ is a subset
    of $n$. For example, notice that $2\in 3$ by the definition of $3$.
    Since $2 = \{0,1\}$ and $3 = \{0,1,2\}$, we have $2\subset 3$.
    \item The set $n$ together with a strict order $\in$ is well-ordered.
    Recall that then we define an order $<=$ in the sense of
    \Cref{remark:ordering-and-strict-order}.
}
Thus one can generalize natural numbers by the following way.

useenv defin [Ordinal Numbers] {
    A set $\alpha$ is an \it{ordinal number}\index{ordinal number} if
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item Every element of $\alpha$ is a subset of $\alpha$.
        \item $\in$ is a strict order of $\alpha$ which is well-ordered.
    }

    We define a \it{successor ordinal}\label{successor ordinal} of $\alpha$ by
    $\alpha + 1 \defeq \alpha\cup \{\alpha\}$.
}

useenv remark {
    By the definition of natural numbers and ordinal numbers,
    every natural number is an ordinal number.
}
